WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:11.828
A storm is coming toward the shore. Dark clouds fill the horizon.

2
00:00:11.829 --> 00:00:15.263
At first it seems to be a large but ordinary storm.

3
00:00:15.264 --> 00:00:18.168
But the rain falls more heavily than usual.

4
00:00:18.169 --> 00:00:25.670
The wind begins to blow faster and faster, more than one hundred kilometers per hour.

5
00:00:25.671 --> 00:00:32.421
Soon the wind shakes the street signs back and forth, as if an invisible giant were trying to pull them out of the Earth.

6
00:00:32.422 --> 00:00:37.235
Trees begin to fall. The force of the wind rips roofs from buildings.

7
00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:40.551
The wind has pushed the ocean waves farther inland.

8
00:00:40.552 --> 00:00:48.842
Suddenly the ocean seems to rise up—as if the sea level were suddenly six meters higher.

9
00:00:48.843 --> 00:00:52.859
The water surges forward and washes away everything that is not secure.

10
00:00:52.860 --> 00:01:00.966
This is a hurricane: a storm that can cover an area 800 kilometers wide and whose clouds can tower 16 kilometers high.

11
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:08.637
Also known as cyclones and typhoons, hurricanes are the largest and most damaging storms on Earth.

12
00:01:08.638 --> 00:01:14.015
The word "hurricane" comes from Hurakan, the name of the Caribbean god of storms.

13
00:01:14.016 --> 00:01:17.805
These giant storms develop in different tropical regions.

14
00:01:17.806 --> 00:01:22.119
The storms from the Caribbean and the Gulf of Mexico are called hurricanes.

15
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:28.423
In India, they are called cyclones, and in the Pacific, they are typhoons.

16
00:01:28.424 --> 00:01:34.725
Hurricanes form in an unusual way. They do not begin as a single storm that slowly grows.

17
00:01:34.726 --> 00:01:38.604
Near the equator, large thunderstorms tend to rotate.

18
00:01:38.605 --> 00:01:45.940
This movement is caused by the Earth's rotation and the regular direction of the Earth's winds near the equator.

19
00:01:45.941 --> 00:01:52.731
If several tropical thunderstorms are close together, they can merge to form a single giant storm called a hurricane.

20
00:01:52.732 --> 00:01:58.306
Hurricanes always rotate. Some rotate clockwise, some counterclockwise.

21
00:01:58.307 --> 00:02:08.097
Hurricanes that rotate clockwise are formed south of the equator; hurricanes that rotate counterclockwise are formed north of the equator.

22
00:02:08.098 --> 00:02:16.481
At first glance, a hurricane may appear to be just a gigantic, swirling storm cloud, but hurricanes have a complicated structure.

23
00:02:16.482 --> 00:02:21.387
A hurricane's winds rotate faster and faster the closer they get to its center.

24
00:02:21.388 --> 00:02:27.208
But right in the middle is the eye of the hurricane, where there is almost no wind, clouds, or rain.

25
00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:32.287
The eye of a hurricane can be up to fifty kilometers wide.

26
00:02:32.288 --> 00:02:37.603
Immediately surrounding the eye of a hurricane is a towering wall of cloud called the eyewall.

27
00:02:37.604 --> 00:02:45.451
Here, the hurricane's wind speed is fastest, so fast that it can produce tornadoes with winds of almost five hundred kilometers per hour.

28
00:02:45.452 --> 00:02:52.365
Hurricanes are formed over areas of warm ocean and are fed by the humid air above the water.

29
00:02:52.366 --> 00:02:57.539
As long as the hurricane stays over the warm ocean water, its strength increases.

30
00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.868
The warm air rises, generating wind and pulling in more humid air to fuel its storm.

31
00:03:02.869 --> 00:03:07.093
When a hurricane moves over land or cool water, it begins to weaken.

32
00:03:07.094 --> 00:03:13.809
But hurricanes are so strong that it can take up to ten days for one to lose its power.

33
00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:18.511
The World Meteorological Organization is responsible for naming hurricanes.

34
00:03:18.512 --> 00:03:24.334
Each year hurricanes are named in alphabetical order, alternating between masculine and feminine names.

35
00:03:24.335 --> 00:03:28.471
Not all the letters of the alphabet are used to begin the name of a hurricane.

36
00:03:28.472 --> 00:03:35.767
Q, U, X, Y, and Z are omitted, leaving only 21 letters of the alphabet available.

37
00:03:35.768 --> 00:03:41.550
If more than 21 hurricanes form in one year, the letters of the Greek alphabet are used.

38
00:03:41.551 --> 00:03:47.388
The twenty-second hurricane would be named Alpha, the twenty-third would be named Beta, and so on.

39
00:03:47.389 --> 00:03:49.905
When a hurricane has caused a terrible tragedy,

40
00:03:49.906 --> 00:03:57.494
a country can request that the name of the hurricane be retired so that no future hurricane will have that name again.

41
00:03:57.495 --> 00:04:03.436
Just as storms can differ in the speed of their winds and the amount of rain they cause, so can hurricanes.

42
00:04:03.437 --> 00:04:08.797
Hurricanes are rated from categories one to five, category five being the strongest.

43
00:04:08.798 --> 00:04:14.024
The system of classifying hurricanes is called the Saffir-Simpson hurricane scale.

44
00:04:14.025 --> 00:04:20.337
Even a category one or category two hurricane can have a disastrous impact.

45
00:04:20.338 --> 00:04:26.155
A slow-moving hurricane can drop a tremendous amount of rain, causing landslides or floods.

46
00:04:26.156 --> 00:04:31.846
But in general, the higher the category number, the more dangerous the hurricane is to property and people.

47
00:04:31.847 --> 00:04:36.588
The high winds of a hurricane can push the surface of the ocean into a tall wave.

48
00:04:36.589 --> 00:04:42.298
When this powerful wave hits the shore, it is called the storm surge.

49
00:04:42.299 --> 00:04:48.066
Wind speed and the level of the storm surge are the primary characteristics used to classify hurricanes.

50
00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:57.264
Hurricanes are dangerous in many ways. People can be killed or injured by the flying debris propelled by their powerful winds.

51
00:04:57.265 --> 00:05:04.799
After the hurricane has passed, many may die from disease, since sanitation systems frequently break down during a hurricane.

52
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.299
But 90 percent of the victims of hurricanes are drowned by the storm surge.

53
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:16.625
Some hurricanes have left 150 kilometers of coastal land submerged underneath 3 meters of water.

54
00:05:16.626 --> 00:05:20.622
Many parts of the world have been repeatedly struck by hurricanes.

55
00:05:20.623 --> 00:05:26.306
With millions of people living in big coastal cities, large populations are at risk.

56
00:05:26.307 --> 00:05:32.131
In response to the threat of hurricanes, governments in these areas have developed evacuation plans.

57
00:05:32.132 --> 00:05:38.971
Unfortunately meteorologists cannot perfectly forecast where and when a hurricane will hit land.

58
00:05:38.972 --> 00:05:46.908
Because it is hard to evacuate whole cities with only a few days' notice, many governments provide hurricane shelters in vulnerable areas.

59
00:05:46.909 --> 00:05:50.699
Sometimes these are large public buildings, such as stadiums.

60
00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:56.858
Other shelters are specifically designed to be used only during hurricanes.

61
00:05:56.859 --> 00:06:04.668
A powerful hurricane can leave chaos in its wake; bridges can be torn down, electricity and sanitation systems can be destroyed.

62
00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:11.379
It is recommended that people who live in areas affected by hurricanes store enough food and drinking water to last several days.

63
00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:14.055
The food should be canned and nonperishable.

64
00:06:14.056 --> 00:06:20.451
It may be a long time before help can arrive, so it is important to be prepared.

65
00:06:20.452 --> 00:06:25.558
Global warming may make coastal regions even more vulnerable to damage by hurricanes.

66
00:06:25.559 --> 00:06:29.593
Hurricanes gain their strength from warm water in tropical regions.

67
00:06:29.594 --> 00:06:33.621
If global warming increases, so will the temperature of the oceans.

68
00:06:33.622 --> 00:06:45.589
Higher temperatures, it is believed, will lead to more hurricanes than before—and these hurricanes are likely to be more powerful.
